@Alexandra -- In the case of the integer sequence consisting of the base-10 digits of Pi, i.e., 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, ..., we could find such substrings as "1, 4, 1" or "4, 1, 5, 9" using consecutive terms -- within the first several terms -- but to find "4, 5, 9" using consecutive terms of the decimal expansion of Pi, we'd have to start with some occurrence of "4" beyond the one that occurs as the 3rd term.

So when you say "It's possible to find any finite subsequence of even numbers" ... then it seems to me that you have to be allowing some subsequences that you "find" to consist of terms of this sequence (A001223) that are *not* consecutive -- i.e., their indices don't have to be consecutive integers.  E.g., if you're saying you could find *any* sequence consisting of a finite number of even numbers, then that includes finding something like "2, 2, 2", which *never* occurs as three consecutive terms in A001223.  In other words, there is no integer j such that A001223(j), A001223(j+1), and A001223(j+2) are 2, 2, and 2, respectively.

So does your conjecture just mean that every even number occurs infinitely many times in A001223?  If it means something else, what does it mean?  As it is, I'm afraid it's much too vague to be included in the Comments section.  :-(

